1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,400
For thousands of years, we found ways to preserve what we knew.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:07,690
From stone,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:08,780
to leaves.

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:11,900
From leaves,

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:13,920
to paper.

6
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,510
Then we learned how to reproduce knowledge.

7
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,140
Then writing became typing.

8
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:25,750
Then we stopped typing,

9
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:27,010
and started touching.

10
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:30,360
And now,

11
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:31,590
we talk.

12
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,700
🎤 “Explain my notes in simple words.”

13
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,570
Every generation changed the way we interact with knowledge.

14
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,990
So what happens when our notebooks can listen too?

15
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,310
Meet Notebook Companion.

16
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,090
Tap the mic once. Then just talk.

17
00:00:52,037 --> 00:00:56,547
🎤 “Plants make their own food using sunlight, water and carbon dioxide.”

18
00:00:59,276 --> 00:01:03,566
Your words become notes. In English, in your own language, or a mix of both.

19
00:01:04,944 --> 00:01:06,974
Need more? Just ask it to write.

20
00:01:09,007 --> 00:01:13,067
🎤 “Write a short summary of photosynthesis, with a heading and a diagram.”

21
00:01:16,938 --> 00:01:20,898
A heading, a summary, even a diagram. Without typing a word.

22
00:01:22,995 --> 00:01:25,185
Change anything, just by saying it.

23
00:01:27,307 --> 00:01:29,607
🎤 “Change the heading to Food From Light.”

24
00:01:31,996 --> 00:01:33,756
Changed your mind? Say undo.

25
00:01:36,396 --> 00:01:37,316
🎤 “Undo that.”

26
00:01:41,629 --> 00:01:45,419
Speak the way you really talk. Hindi and English, in the same sentence.

27
